<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements. Notarization rejects any build that is not
  signed with the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime then denies the
  four things the BEAM cannot run without. Each key below is a hole punched back
  through it on purpose.

  NOTE FOR EDITORS: no double hyphen may appear anywhere in these comments. It
  is illegal inside an XML comment, and while `plutil -lint` accepts it,
  codesign's stricter AMFI parser rejects the whole file with
  "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line N" and signs nothing. That is why
  command flags are spelled out in words here rather than quoted literally.

  This file is referenced from TWO places, deliberately pointed at the same path
  so they can never drift:

    tauri.conf.json, at bundle.macOS.entitlements  (the .app itself)
    scripts/codesign_release.sh                    (every Mach-O in the OTP tree)

  The second is the whole reason this file is not just the shell's business.
  ENTITLEMENTS DO NOT INHERIT ACROSS PROCESS BOUNDARIES. `beam.smp` is spawned
  as a separate process out of Contents/Resources (see desktop/tauri/src/main.rs,
  resolve_release_binary) and does NOT inherit the shell's entitlements. It must
  carry these on its own signature. Sign the ERTS without them and the failure
  takes the worst shape available: notarization passes, the DMG ships, and the
  VM dies at launch on the user's machine. It fails nowhere in our pipeline and
  everywhere in theirs.

  The set matches Livebook's, a Tauri v2 app shipping an Elixir/OTP release,
  i.e. the same architecture, already notarized and in the wild. See
  daily-growth/roadmaps/LAUNCH_ROADMAP.md section III.E.

  NOTHING ELSE BELONGS HERE. In particular com.apple.security.get-task-allow
  (the debug entitlement) is a hard notary failure and must never appear.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- BEAM has had a real JIT (BeamAsm) since OTP 24. It needs W^X executable
       mappings, which the hardened runtime refuses by default. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Required in practice alongside allow-jit for BEAM's code allocation.
       BeamAsm's allocator does not confine itself to the MAP_JIT regions that
       allow-jit alone permits. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Load-bearing, and the one most likely to be dropped as redundant. The
       BEAM calls dlopen on its NIFs at runtime: crypto.so, sqlite3_nif.so,
       asn1rt_nif.so and the rest. To the loader a NIF is a plug-in from another
       team, and library validation rejects it. Seven of those .so files do not
       even carry an execute bit, which is why codesign_release.sh finds Mach-O
       objects by content rather than by mode. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- OTP's launcher scripts rely on dyld behaviour driven by DYLD_ environment
       variables, which the hardened runtime scrubs unless this is set. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
